summary:
## The Problem: Managing Many Routes

For applications with many nested pages, like a documentation site, creating a file for every route is inefficient. A site with 20 features and 20 concepts per feature could require **400 separate route files**. Using standard dynamic routes (`[featureId]`, `[conceptId]`) reduces this but adds folder nesting for each URL segment, which can become cumbersome.

## Solution: Catch-all Segments

Next.js offers **catch-all segments** to capture multiple path segments with a single file. This is perfect for content-heavy sites with a consistent layout.

- **File Structure**: Create a folder with three dots and a parameter name inside square brackets.
- **Example**: `app/docs/[...slug]/page.tsx`

This single `page.tsx` will now handle routes like:
- `/docs/feature-1`
- `/docs/feature-1/concept-1`
- `/docs/feature-1/concept-1/example-1`

> [!TIP]
The name `slug` is a common convention for URL segments, but you can choose any name.

## Accessing Dynamic Segments

Inside the page component, the captured segments are available via the `params` prop as an array of strings.

```typescript
// app/docs/[...slug]/page.tsx

export default function DocsPage({ params }: {
  params: { slug: string[] }
}) {
  if (params.slug?.length === 2) {
    return <h1>Viewing docs for {params.slug[0]} and {params.slug[1]}</h1>;
  }
  if (params.slug?.length === 1) {
    return <h1>Viewing docs for {params.slug[0]}</h1>;
  }

  return <h1>Docs Home Page</h1>;
}
```

## Optional Catch-all Segments

By default, a catch-all route like `[...slug]` does not match the base path (e.g., `/docs`). To include the base path, use **optional catch-all segments** by wrapping the folder name in an additional set of square brackets.

- **File Structure**: `app/docs/[[...slug]]/page.tsx`

This structure will match `/docs` in addition to all sub-paths.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> When using optional catch-all segments, the `params.slug` property can be `undefined` when the base route is matched. Always use optional chaining (`params.slug?.length`) or other checks to prevent runtime errors.
